
The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) is the third SOLID principle.

It states: "Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without affecting the correctness of the program."

The problem LSP solves: Prevents subclasses from breaking the behavior expected from the base class, ensuring that derived classes extend base classes without changing their intended behavior.


Summary:

LSP solves the problem where subclasses override or extend base class methods in a way that violates the expectations or contracts of the base class.

This prevents bugs and unexpected behavior when using polymorphism, making code more robust and maintainable.

Example: If a subclass throws exceptions or changes method semantics, code using the base class may break when a subclass is substituted. LSP ensures this does not happen.

Violation:
    1. Show a violation of the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) using a Bird example.
    2. Problem: Subclass Penguin cannot fly, but base class Bird has a fly() method.
    3. Substituting Penguin for Bird breaks expected behavior.


    // Java
    public class Bird {
        public void fly() {
            System.out.println("Bird is flying");
        }
    }

    // Java
    public class Sparrow extends Bird {
        @Override
        public void fly() {
            System.out.println("Sparrow is flying");
        }
    }

    // Java
    public class Penguin extends Bird {
        @Override
        public void fly() {
            throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Penguin can't fly");
        }
    }

    // Java
    public class BirdWatcher {
        public void observe(Bird bird) {
            System.out.println("BirdWatcher: Let's watch the bird fly!");
            bird.fly(); // Assumes all birds can fly
        }
    }

    // Java
    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            BirdWatcher watcher = new BirdWatcher();

            Bird sparrow = new Sparrow();
            watcher.observe(sparrow); // OK

            Bird penguin = new Penguin();
            watcher.observe(penguin); // Throws exception: violates LSP
        }
    }

This design violates LSP because substituting a Penguin for a Bird causes unexpected exceptions.
A better design would avoid forcing all birds to implement fly(), or use interfaces for flying birds only.


---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fix:
    Use interfaces for behaviors (Flyable, Swimmable).
    Use a base class Bird for common properties.
    Use dependency injection and polymorphism to avoid explicit instanceof checks.
    Create a BirdService class to process birds and invoke their behaviors.
    Use a list of birds and process them in a clean, extensible way.


    // Java
    public abstract class Bird {
        public void eat() {
            System.out.println(getClass().getSimpleName() + " is eating");
        }
    }

    // Java
    public interface Flyable {
        void fly();
    }

    // Java
    public interface Swimmable {
        void swim();
    }

    // Java
    public class Sparrow extends Bird implements Flyable {
        @Override
        public void fly() {
            System.out.println("Sparrow is flying");
        }
    }

    // Java
    public class Penguin extends Bird implements Swimmable {
        @Override
        public void swim() {
            System.out.println("Penguin is swimming");
        }
    }


    // Java
    public class BirdService {
        public void processBird(Bird bird) {
            bird.eat();

            if (bird instanceof Flyable flyable) {
                flyable.fly();
            }

            if (bird instanceof Swimmable swimmable) {
                swimmable.swim();
            }
        }
    }

    // Java
    public class BirdDemo {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            BirdService service = new BirdService();

            Bird sparrow = new Sparrow();
            Bird penguin = new Penguin();

            service.processBird(sparrow);
            service.processBird(penguin);
        }
    }

